1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,480
The JVM arc taught you what happens under
a running program. Now shift the camera.
Two teammates argue about a redesign.

2
00:00:07,600 --> 00:00:20,199
One says, "We need a Strategy here." The
other says, "That is just an interface." A
third says, "Patterns are outdated — use
Spring." They are missing a shared
definition.

3
00:00:20,320 --> 00:00:33,679
Design patterns are shared vocabulary for
recurring design problems — context,
forces, and trade-offs — not stamps you
smash onto every class to sound senior.
Look at this java for a moment.

4
00:00:33,799 --> 00:00:48,280
A pattern is useful when the problem
actually recurs — when naming it saves
meeting time. If the problem appears once,
a clear function may beat a named pattern.
Prefer clarity. Do not pattern-paint
simple code.

5
00:00:48,399 --> 00:00:58,679
Start from a situation. You are building
pricing rules. Today there is one rule.
Tomorrow marketing wants seasonal,
loyalty, and partner discounts. Nested
conditionals grow.

6
00:00:58,799 --> 00:01:04,000
Someone proposes a Strategy hierarchy;
someone else a switch; someone else a
rules engine.

7
00:01:04,120 --> 00:01:10,560
The conversation is not "which buzzword?"
It is "what problem are we solving, what
trade-offs do we accept,

8
00:01:10,680 --> 00:01:19,280
and will the next person recognize this
shape?" Context plus problem plus trade-
offs — that is how catalogs like Gang of
Four earned their keep.

9
00:01:19,400 --> 00:01:30,920
Named experience, not holy scripture. Hold
a second situation beside pricing. You
inherit a payment integration duplicated
in three modules with different timeouts
and retries.

10
00:01:31,040 --> 00:01:40,320
Someone says "Adapter." Someone says
"Facade." Someone says "just copy the best
one into a util." Vocabulary helps only if
you can say:

11
00:01:40,439 --> 00:01:49,359
we need one curated door over a messy
subsystem — facade energy — or we need to
translate a foreign interface into our
port — adapter energy.

12
00:01:49,479 --> 00:01:59,679
Without the problem statement, pattern
names become team theater. Language
features can replace some classical
patterns. A Java enum with methods can
replace certain constant-heavy singletons.

13
00:01:59,799 --> 00:02:10,399
Lambdas shrink some Strategy boilerplate.
Records shrink tiny data carriers. Modules
and DI containers change how factories
appear. That does not make patterns
outdated.

14
00:02:10,519 --> 00:02:24,519
Catalogs evolve; naming recurring
structures still helps. Before lambdas,
Strategy often meant a named class per
variant; after, many Strategies become a
one-liner — same idea, thinner ceremony.

15
00:02:24,639 --> 00:02:33,319
"Patterns are outdated" is too blunt;
"always use the GoF book literally" is too
blunt the other way. Read patterns as
tools in a drawer.

16
00:02:33,439 --> 00:02:45,520
You reach for a wrench when a bolt needs
turning — not empty the drawer onto every
repair. Factory methods help when creation
deserves a name. Decorators help when you
add behavior without exploding subclasses.

17
00:02:45,639 --> 00:02:57,879
Observers help when many listeners react
to an event. Force a pattern where a
straightforward class would do and you pay
indirection tax without gaining
vocabulary. Watch how pattern talk goes
wrong.

18
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:06,240
Someone labels every interface a Strategy.
Someone introduces an AbstractFactory for
two concrete types that will never
multiply.

19
00:03:06,360 --> 00:03:20,480
The cure is the same discipline we used
for JVM flags: name the symptom, name the
trade-off, then choose the tool. Teach a
three-question gate before merging a
pattern: What recurs? What trade-off do we
accept?

20
00:03:20,660 --> 00:03:31,780
What simpler language feature did we
consider first? If those answers are thin,
keep the clear code and drop the pattern
name from the PR. A healthy team uses
pattern names as compression.

21
00:03:31,900 --> 00:03:42,300
"This is a facade over the payment SDK"
communicates intent faster than a five-
minute tour — if everyone shares the
meaning. Misleading names are worse than
no names.

22
00:03:42,420 --> 00:03:52,180
In interviews, when asked whether patterns
still matter, answer with team compression
and evolving catalogs: one example where a
language feature replaced a classical
shape,

23
00:03:52,300 --> 00:03:56,220
and one where naming Strategy or Decorator
still clarifies a review.

24
00:03:56,340 --> 00:04:00,180
Today we demystified patterns as
vocabulary under constraints.

25
00:04:00,300 --> 00:04:12,860
Next we get concrete with creational
patterns — how objects come to life, where
singletons go wrong, when builders beat
telescoping constructors, and how
containers often own lifecycle in modern
Java.
